WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:01.939
Film Courage: You are sitting in traffic on
Wilshire Boulevard.

00:00:01.939 --> 00:00:07.024
I’m sure it was a lovely Los Angeles intersection
with lots of commotion [recounting how he

00:00:07.024 --> 00:00:09.580
developed the idea for his feature scifi film
AFTER WE LEAVE], was this your first feature

00:00:09.580 --> 00:00:10.950
script that you were planning to write?

00:00:10.950 --> 00:00:12.230
Or had you written prior feature scripts?

00:00:12.230 --> 00:00:13.230
Aleem Hossain, filmmaker and AR/VR creator,
assistant professor in media arts and culture

00:00:13.230 --> 00:00:15.420
at Occidental College: I had written many
feature scripts.

00:00:15.420 --> 00:00:19.550
Most of them I had written right after I got
out of film school.

00:00:19.550 --> 00:00:23.710
Largely with an eye for trying to sell them
or get them set up to be produced sort of

00:00:23.710 --> 00:00:27.210
within the studio system, a few of which I
even liked.

00:00:27.210 --> 00:00:30.870
Some of them I’m glad I wrote just because
I think you need to write a certain amount

00:00:30.870 --> 00:00:33.820
of bad scripts just to get to a better script.

00:00:33.820 --> 00:00:40.280
But yes I had been writing a lot and with
a few things that I had been well-received,

00:00:40.280 --> 00:00:45.570
I was still running up against the roadblock
of people saying this is a potentially makable

00:00:45.570 --> 00:00:47.730
script but not with you as a director.

00:00:47.730 --> 00:00:50.020
You’ve never directed a feature.

00:00:50.020 --> 00:00:52.160
No one is going to take that risk.

00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:56.710
And so AFTER WE LEAVE was my answer to that
recurring complaint.

00:00:56.710 --> 00:01:00.679
I was like “Fine, if that is the case I’m
just going to go make my own movie and get

00:01:00.679 --> 00:01:02.120
that one excuse out of the way.

00:01:02.120 --> 00:01:07.360
I will be a feature filmmaker by making my
own film and then I can hopefully return and

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:09.130
get something bigger budget off of the ground.”

00:01:09.130 --> 00:01:12.180
Film Courage: And these were producers that
you were pitching that were saying this?

00:01:12.180 --> 00:01:17.530
Aleem: Producers, agents and never by the
way in a mean way.

00:01:17.530 --> 00:01:23.090
I think appreciate of the work that I was
trying to do and interested in the stories

00:01:23.090 --> 00:01:24.930
that I wanted to tell.

00:01:24.930 --> 00:01:30.229
But just being frank about the reality of
who they were going to entrust “X” dollars

00:01:30.229 --> 00:01:33.990
with and that it would be a hard fight for
someone who hadn’t made a feature.

00:01:33.990 --> 00:01:37.660
Which is sort of a catch-22 right?

00:01:37.660 --> 00:01:41.510
We talk a lot about access to the industry
and if the access point is how come you haven’t

00:01:41.510 --> 00:01:44.300
already made something we get caught in this
vicious circle.

00:01:44.300 --> 00:01:47.719
And so I just wanted to break out of that.

00:01:47.719 --> 00:01:52.610
For me the solution was just making a very
small budgeted film that I could direct and

00:01:52.610 --> 00:01:59.159
craft in the way that I wanted which again
I did primarily as a form of self-expression.

00:01:59.159 --> 00:02:04.720
I made the movie not as a career calling card
primarily, not as a strategic movie in Hollywood.

00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:07.869
I made it because I just wanted to tell that
story.

00:02:07.869 --> 00:02:13.450
I think the benefit is it now makes me a director
in a lot of people’s eyes and it has opened

00:02:13.450 --> 00:02:17.510
more doors but it wasn’t really my main
reason for doing it.

00:02:17.510 --> 00:02:22.349
Film Courage: Speaking of access…and I know
we are taking a little bit of a…we are diverging

00:02:22.349 --> 00:02:23.349
a bit.

00:02:23.349 --> 00:02:24.349
Aleem: That’s fine.

00:02:24.349 --> 00:02:29.200
Film Courage: What are some of the misconceptions
whether they are from students or other people

00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:35.210
in the industry about access and what have
you seen from going through this process yourself,

00:02:35.210 --> 00:02:37.120
being told this yourself that you can’t
do it.

00:02:37.120 --> 00:02:42.580
Aleem: I think if we are specifically talking
about feature films, I think the biggest problem

00:02:42.580 --> 00:02:48.970
with access is that the business entirely
has changed since a lot of us fell in love

00:02:48.970 --> 00:02:50.069
with movies.

00:02:50.069 --> 00:02:54.659
There are just so many fewer smaller movies
being made but the studios and the big production

00:02:54.659 --> 00:02:59.650
companies that I have to think that opportunities
for new directors or less experienced directors

00:02:59.650 --> 00:03:00.709
aren’t even there.

00:03:00.709 --> 00:03:04.989
I actually think that a lot of that access
problem is they are not in the business of

00:03:04.989 --> 00:03:07.700
making those movies that they used to give
to a younger director.

00:03:07.700 --> 00:03:10.580
They are just making these huge movies.

00:03:10.580 --> 00:03:16.349
That being said I definitely think for sure
the overall industry has biases against and

00:03:16.349 --> 00:03:21.310
they are structural biases, they are biases
that come not from necessarily bad intentions

00:03:21.310 --> 00:03:26.580
but there are barriers to women, to people
of color, for sure.

00:03:26.580 --> 00:03:31.049
I think the hope right is that there is an
antidote to that where we can now make our

00:03:31.049 --> 00:03:32.049
own content.

00:03:32.049 --> 00:03:34.220
And I do think that that is a valid idea.

00:03:34.220 --> 00:03:37.069
That the way around the access problem is
to make stuff.

00:03:37.069 --> 00:03:41.330
But I think the funny thing these days is
I don’t know if the model anymore is make

00:03:41.330 --> 00:03:46.150
a low-budget film so you can then jump up
to a lower mid-budget studio movie.

00:03:46.150 --> 00:03:48.709
Because those don’t even exists.

00:03:48.709 --> 00:03:52.770
I think that really I have sort of re-thought
how I think about my career.

00:03:52.770 --> 00:03:54.590
I’m just going to keep making movies.

00:03:54.590 --> 00:03:59.500
I don’t actually expect there to be this
moment where all of the sudden where now I

00:03:59.500 --> 00:04:04.610
don’t have to self-produce or at least partially
self-produce my own movies because I don’t

00:04:04.610 --> 00:04:06.209
know if that path exists anymore.

00:04:06.209 --> 00:04:09.810
I really think that if I want to make these
movies then I will make them.

00:04:09.810 --> 00:04:14.939
The career of directing movies in Hollywood
there are so few films made these days that

00:04:14.939 --> 00:04:19.280
I don’t know that that is a path that one
sort of incrementally gets to.

00:04:19.280 --> 00:04:22.450
Sometimes you make a film good enough on your
own and you get that lightning strike and

00:04:22.450 --> 00:04:24.580
you can level up in a big way.

00:04:24.580 --> 00:04:28.900
But I don’t think there are more incremental
steps because I think there is nothing in

00:04:28.900 --> 00:04:29.900
between.

00:04:29.900 --> 00:04:36.040
Film Courage: When your students come to you
with questions about access are you telling

00:04:36.040 --> 00:04:38.130
them the same thing basically?

00:04:38.130 --> 00:04:41.960
Don’t try to make something just so you
can get to that next level so someone can

00:04:41.960 --> 00:04:42.960
“endorse” you?

00:04:42.960 --> 00:04:46.510
Aleem: I mean the very first thing that we
talk about at Oxy [Occidental College in Los

00:04:46.510 --> 00:04:51.550
Angeles, California] that the skills involved
in filmmaking and media making we don’t

00:04:51.550 --> 00:04:54.220
think of those as skills that are inclusively
for Hollywood.

00:04:54.220 --> 00:05:00.060
I think we live in a world where the ability
to tell your story and express yourself visually

00:05:00.060 --> 00:05:03.020
is something that everyone needs to do.

00:05:03.020 --> 00:05:09.200
Individuals, companies, non-profits, governments,
cultural institutions, all of them need to

00:05:09.200 --> 00:05:10.200
do that.

00:05:10.200 --> 00:05:14.571
So I think of this toolbox (artistically and
logistically) that we are giving people in

00:05:14.571 --> 00:05:18.800
the classroom as applying to a huge amount
of life.

00:05:18.800 --> 00:05:23.220
When we talk specifically about access to
Hollywood I do tell people that I don’t

00:05:23.220 --> 00:05:27.970
think that film director is a career.

00:05:27.970 --> 00:05:30.050
I think film director is an aspiration.

00:05:30.050 --> 00:05:34.200
I think independent film director is a calling
or I wish I had a better word than hobby because

00:05:34.200 --> 00:05:35.660
I don’t think it’s a hobby.

00:05:35.660 --> 00:05:37.000
But I don’t actually think it’s a career
path.

00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:39.760
I think it is a thing one does because you
are compelled to do it.

00:05:39.760 --> 00:05:45.370
If you were to corner even who you think of
as a successful independent director and ask

00:05:45.370 --> 00:05:49.410
them “Do you pay your mortgage with filmmaking
income this year?”

00:05:49.410 --> 00:05:52.810
So many of them will say no and that is just
the reality.

00:05:52.810 --> 00:05:54.660
That is not a reason to stop making movies.

00:05:54.660 --> 00:05:58.060
It’s a reason to ask myself “Why am I
making movies?”

00:05:58.060 --> 00:06:01.790
If you are making movies because you just
feel compelled to do it, that is the right

00:06:01.790 --> 00:06:01.860
answer.

